From 6f7951ff1718390bfba0837798bf7e73dcc0a743 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:15:20 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] The cameraman keeps out of bodies and the world, holds its
 shots, and stops at the fight's end (patch 0089)

Five faults from filming every Deadmines boss with `WOW_SPECTATE` (patch 0088) on Nov 9:

- The camera went inside models (the Shredder's grind drove over its spot; Rhahk'Zor, Cookie,
  Gilnid and VanCleef too). The eye now keeps out of every body's bounds (its model's Stand box:
  half its diagonal from the selection ring's footprint, and its height) by 1.25 yd and four
  tenths of its radius, the boss's, the hero's and anyone's near them, and out of where each
  body's measured walk takes it in the next 0.9 s: a boss charging at the spot is a cut before it
  arrives. The eye no longer pulls in among the bodies to see past one.
- A solid teal frame (outside the world). Every position has solid ground below it, the world
  loaded round it, and a clear line from the point it looks at; no ground under the eye for
  0.15 s is a cut; a move that would sweep through the world between two positions is a cut
  instead; and a teleport goes straight to the shot's pose rather than holding a stale spot.
- Too many cuts in small rooms (20 in 32 s on Marisa). A position is held at least 3 s; before
  that only a body about to cover the camera, the eye out of the world, or both of the two hidden
  for 0.3 s moves it. One of them is hidden only when 3 of 5 points (low, chest, head, either
  side) can't be seen, so a thin post or a crate in front doesn't count; a body in the way counts
  by six tenths of its width, since limbs have gaps. A cut goes only to a clearly better
  position, and a position with no room just now holds the eye where it is.
- Foreground clutter. Positions are scored: points of the two hidden, the world across the near
  part of the frame, an eye pulled in, then the shot's order; the clearest wins.
- Dead footage. When the hero falls, or the subject (the last of its forms, `Cookie|Gorged`)
  does, it logs `the fight is over: <name> fell` (the rig ends the video on it) and holds where it
  is rather than following the hero to a lamp, until the hero has been up beside a living subject
  for 5 s. Each log line carries its time (`t=12.3`).
